Ah, interesting! In this specific instance I'm using lodash (latest release). Like so:

```javascript
const _ = require('lodash');
const s = Symbol('s')
const headers = { [s]: 'value' };
_.omitBy(headers, (v, k) => k.startsWith(':')); // k.startsWith is not a function
```

I can't speak for the reasoning on their end, but it seems that they include enumerable symbols in all methods like this intentionally, and they have done for many years: https://github.com/lodash/lodash/blob/master/.internal/getAllKeysIn.js

This does work fine if `sensitiveHeaders` is set as a non-enumerable property, like so:

```javascript
const _ = require('lodash');
const s = Symbol('s')
const headers = { };
Object.defineProperty(headers, s, {
  value: 'value',
  enumerable: false
});
_.omitBy(headers, (v, k) => k.startsWith(':')); // All good
```

I'm not sure whether lodash is correct here, but I think there's an argument that others will hit this, and since conceptually it shouldn't be considered as a header key, there's an argument that this shouldn't be an enumerable property.

